Correct: your code correctly uses a hash map to count existing numbers and finds the missing ones in linear time, but it uses extra space.


2. REVIEW MY CODE

* What I did well: You correctly used an unordered map to store the numbers, and you properly checked for missing keys using ust.find(i) == ust.end(), which fixes the iterator comparison issue from your earlier drafts.


* Is it correct: Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases.
* Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. However, because you only need to know if a number exists, using a map to count frequencies is unnecessary. A hash set would do the exact same job but is slightly cleaner because it only stores the keys.
* Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(n) because you loop through the array twice, doing average constant-time map operations. Space complexity is O(n) auxiliary space because your hash map stores all the unique numbers from the array.
* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: You record every number present in the array into your map. Then, you check every expected number from 1 to N. If a number is not found in the map, you know it is missing and you add it to your answer list.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Hash Set Memory (Your Approach Refined)

* Where it comes from: Common beginner approach and standard dictionary pattern.
* Idea: Instead of a map that stores keys and counts, use a set which only stores unique keys. Drop all numbers from the array into the set. Then loop from 1 to N and check which ones are missing.
* C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findDisappearedNumbers(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> numSet(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        vector<int> ans;
        for(int i = 1; i <= nums.size(); i++){
            if(numSet.find(i) == numSet.end()){
                ans.push_back(i);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) to build the set and check values. Space complexity is O(n) auxiliary space to store the set.
* What makes the next approach better: Hash sets use extra memory. We can achieve O(1) auxiliary space by modifying the input array in-place, which is the optimal solution for this specific problem.



Approach 2: In-Place Array Marking (Optimal)

* Where it comes from: LeetCode official editorial and competitive programming tricks for numbers bound between 1 and N.
* Idea: Since the numbers are always between 1 and N, we can use the values themselves to point to array indexes. When we see a number like 4, we go to index 3 (which is 4 minus 1) and make the number sitting there negative. A negative number acts as a marked flag saying "we saw the number corresponding to this index". At the end, any index that still has a positive number means its corresponding number was never seen.
