* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) because we do two linear passes. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space because we reuse the input array for our flags.
* What makes the next approach better: This is the absolute best approach for this problem because it meets the strict O(1) extra space follow-up requirement.

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use: You used a Hash Map, which is functionally very close to Approach 1.
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem: They expect the In-Place Array Marking approach. The problem description usually explicitly asks if you can do it without extra space.
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
Question: Why do we use the absolute value when marking the array?
Answer: Because a number might get marked as negative early on, but we still need its original value later in the loop to find which index it points to. Taking the absolute value gives us back the original number safely.

5. WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

* The key insight, in one sentence: When an array of size N contains values strictly between 1 and N, you can use the values as indexes to mark visited spots in the exact same array.
* My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Remember that when you only need to check existence, unordered_set is better than unordered_map. Also, read the problem constraints carefully; if it asks for no extra space, look for an in-place array modification trick.
* The pattern or idea this question is really about: Array In-Place Hashing and Value-as-Index mapping.

